<Project>

  <!-- The pinned BepInEx build, shared with build-support.psm1. -->
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)BepInEx.props" />

  <!-- The pinned protobuf packages, shared with build-support.psm1. -->
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Protobuf.props" />

  <!--
    Keep every generated file under one gitignored .build\ folder at the repo
    root, instead of scattering bin\ and obj\ through src\. The PowerShell build
    scripts put their staging and dist folders there too.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <BuildRootDir>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory).build\</BuildRootDir>
    <BaseOutputPath>$(BuildRootDir)bin\$(MSBuildProjectName)\</BaseOutputPath>
    <BaseIntermediateOutputPath>$(BuildRootDir)obj\$(MSBuildProjectName)\</BaseIntermediateOutputPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    WHICH CARGO PROFILE'S OUTPUT A C# BUILD COPIES. Two projects take the look-ahead engine
    and the state library out of target\, and cargo puts each profile's output in a folder
    of its own, so the folder is a property rather than a path spelled three times.

    RELEASE BY DEFAULT, because that is what a bare `dotnet build` or `dotnet test` should
    find: the profile the measurements are taken with and the one a release ships. The
    scripts at the root override it - build.ps1 and deploy.ps1 with release-incremental,
    which is the test loop's profile and what an iterate step wants, make-release.ps1 with
    release. See Invoke-NativeBuild in build-support.psm1, which builds what this then
    copies.

    IT IS THE FOLDER NAME AND NOT THE PROFILE NAME, which differ for exactly one profile:
    cargo's `dev` writes to target\debug.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RustProfileDir Condition="'$(RustProfileDir)' == ''">release</RustProfileDir>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    A WARNING FAILS THE BUILD, for every project rather than for the plugin alone, which had
    said so in its own csproj since it existed. The Rust side says the same thing in
    Cargo.toml's [workspace.lints.rust], and the reasoning is written out there: a warning
    nobody has to clear is one that accumulates, and a tree with forty of them is one where
    the forty-first is invisible.

    The cost, which is real: an analyzer that ships a new rule can fail a build for somebody
    who changed nothing. It is a short fix for one developer - suppress the rule here, clear
    it, take the suppression out - and cheaper than a count that only ever rises. See de-g6ml.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    What `dotnet test` puts in the test host's environment, so that a bare `dotnet test`
    carries it and no command line has to. See test.runsettings for what is in there and
    why. Set for every project rather than for the test ones: a project that runs no tests
    reads no run settings, so naming them would be a list to keep in step for nothing.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RunSettingsFilePath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)test.runsettings</RunSettingsFilePath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Where the build reads its BepInEx and Il2CppInterop reference assemblies
    from. Three projects need them - Persistence, Session and the Plugin - and
    resolving it here rather than in each csproj means one order, one cache and
    one error message for all of them.

    Resolution order, first hit wins:

      1. -p:DiscoElysiumDir=<path>   (a command-line property beats anything set here)
      2. the DEGCT_GAME_DIR environment variable
      3. the machine-level cache the PowerShell scripts write

    EVERY candidate is checked for BepInEx\core rather than merely for
    existing. The cache is a file naming a directory, and the directory can be
    deleted without the file noticing; without the check a bare 'dotnet build'
    fails on a path that no longer exists while a perfectly good install sits
    elsewhere on the machine. A game without BepInEx cannot supply a single one
    of the assemblies these projects reference, so existing is not enough.

    Step 3 is what makes git worktrees work: the cache is per-user and lives
    outside the repo (see $RefDirCacheFile in build-support.psm1), so one
    .\provision-refs.ps1 or .\build.ps1 anywhere on the machine answers the
    question for every checkout on it. It sits where the PowerShell order puts
    Steam discovery, and stands in for it: MSBuild cannot read Steam's library
    folders, so a cached answer is the only way a build from here ever reaches
    the Steam copy - which makes the PowerShell scripts the way to recover when
    nothing here resolves.

    None of these values may be wrapped across lines: MSBuild trims only the
    ends of a property value, so a line break inside one lands in the middle of
    the path and every Exists() check on it then fails.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RefGameDirCacheFile Condition="'$(LOCALAPPDATA)' != ''">$(LOCALAPPDATA)\GlobalConversationTracker\reference-game-dir.txt</RefGameDirCacheFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <_CachedGameDir Condition="'$(RefGameDirCacheFile)' != '' and Exists('$(RefGameDirCacheFile)')">$([System.IO.File]::ReadAllText('$(RefGameDirCacheFile)').Trim())</_CachedGameDir>
    <DiscoElysiumDir Condition="'$(DiscoElysiumDir)' == '' and '$(DEGCT_GAME_DIR)' != ''">$(DEGCT_GAME_DIR)</DiscoElysiumDir>
    <DiscoElysiumDir Condition="'$(DiscoElysiumDir)' == '' and '$(_CachedGameDir)' != '' and Exists('$(_CachedGameDir)\BepInEx\core')">$(_CachedGameDir)</DiscoElysiumDir>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The two halves of what a build references, which come from different places
    and are worth keeping straight:

      BepInEx\core     BepInEx itself, Harmony, Il2CppInterop. Published as an
                       archive, identical on every machine, so it can simply be
                       DOWNLOADED - see the EnsureBepInExCore target, which
                       fetches the pinned build into the cache the first time
                       anything needs it. Persistence and Session reference
                       nothing else, so they need no game install at all.

      BepInEx\interop  Assembly-CSharp, DialogueSystem, UnityEngine.*, and the
                       rest of the game as IL2CPP interop assemblies. GENERATED
                       on the machine, by BepInEx, out of the game's own
                       GameAssembly.dll, the first time the game is launched
                       with BepInEx installed. There is nowhere to download
                       these from and no copy that is right for every game
                       build, which is why the plugin - and only the plugin -
                       still needs a real install.

    An install that has core is preferred over the download, so that a build
    references the same BepInEx the game beside it is running rather than two
    versions that merely happen to be pinned together.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <BepInExCoreDir Condition="'$(DiscoElysiumDir)' != '' and Exists('$(DiscoElysiumDir)\BepInEx\core')">$(DiscoElysiumDir)\BepInEx\core\</BepInExCoreDir>
    <BepInExCoreDir Condition="'$(BepInExCoreDir)' == '' and '$(BepInExUnpackedDir)' != ''">$(BepInExUnpackedDir)BepInEx\core\</BepInExCoreDir>
    <BepInExInteropDir>$(DiscoElysiumDir)\BepInEx\interop\</BepInExInteropDir>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
